 of exactly this statement. Lean status per paper is kept in authored/evidence-status.json.","citation_ids":["cite.long.prop-critical-band-index"],"evidence_ids":["mapping.long.prop-critical-band-index"],"id":"long.prop-critical-band-index","kind":"claim","label":"Long record Proposition 10.16 (Quantifier collapse: one critical index suffices, not $`d{+}1`$)","locator":"Long record Proposition 10.16, p. 119, label prop:critical-band-index","source_id":"paper.long.pdf","statement":"*Let $d,E\\in\\mathbb N$ and assume $E\\le2^{d+1}$. Then the purely combinatorial statement $\\mathsf{DyadicBandEscape}(d,E) \\iff \\exists j,\\ \\mathsf{CriticalDyadicBandIndex}(d,E,j)\\land\nE+2(d+j)\\le 2^{d-j+1}$ collapses the $\\forall j\\in[0,d]$ band-avoidance condition (formally $d{+}1$ separate inequalities) to checking exactly *one* nearest-boundary index $j$. The range assumption ensures that a dyadic threshold lies at or above $E$. Choose the smallest such threshold: smaller thresholds are already below $E$, while larger thresholds have narrower forbidden bands. Without the range assumption all bands escape automatically when $E>2^{d+1}$, but no critical index exists; $(d,E)=(0,3)$ is the smallest example. Specialised to the concrete seam reset charge, $\\mathsf{SeamUpperResetCriticalBandEscape}$ is proved logically *equivalent* to Definition 10.15’s hypothesis. Zero Mersenne/seam content in the core lemma ; pure $(d,E,j)$ arithmetic over powers of 2.*","status":"unverified"},"ref":"sha256:264b08973a5f23b2f98ad989b0c1708096513d86929f19048623a2a9fe18092c/nodes/long.prop-critical-band-index","relations":[{"derived":false,"family":"arguments","from":"sha256:264b08973a5f23b2f98ad989b0c1708096513d86929f19048623a2a9fe18092c/nodes/long.prop-critical-band-index","id":"sha256:264b08973a5f23b2f98ad989b0c1708096513d86929f19048623a2a9fe18092c/edges/%5B%22long.prop-critical-band-index%22%2C%22lean.ErdosProblems.Erdos257.PaperCompleteR21.paper_critical_band_index_collapse%22%2C%22formalizes%